/* GENERATED by prep.py from states.json — do not edit by hand. */
window.LAWMAP_DATA = {"last_updated":"2026-07-31","maintainer":"Jay Kim — corrections welcome; an unsourced claim is a bug.","baseline_note":"Every tag is measured against ONE baseline: California SB 53 (TFAIA). 'copies-SB53' means the enacted text tracks CA. 'frontier-advancing' means it goes beyond CA on an axis AI resilience advocates care about. 'weaker' means it falls short of CA. 'baseline' is CA itself. 'orthogonal' means the provision is not on the SB 53 axis at all, so comparing it to CA would not be meaningful.","trajectory_note":"`tag` describes the ENACTED text. `trajectory` describes how the text got there. They are orthogonal: a provision can copy SB 53 today AND have been ratcheted down to get there. New York is the case that forced the split.","categories":[{"category":"frontier-ai-framework","label":"Frontier AI framework (write, publish, follow)","definition":"The developer must write, publish, and follow a frontier safety framework describing how it manages catastrophic risk. SB 53 implements the California Report on Frontier AI Policy's transparency recommendation as a requirement for large AI developers to write, publish, and follow safety and security protocols to manage the most severe risks. Rather than prescribe specific technical standards that companies must follow, SB 53 simply requires companies to be transparent about the approaches they are using.","strongest_state":"IL","provision_cite":"IL SB 315 (framework requirement, effective 2028-01-01)","rationale":"All three frontier states require a published framework on materially the same 10-topic template. Illinois is strongest because it is the only one where the framework is verified by a mandated independent third-party audit rather than self-attested — the obligation has an external check.","confidence":"high","definition_source":"Encode pillar language, adapted: Encode AI et al., SB 53 coalition letter to Gov. Newsom (23 signatory organizations), 2025-09-24 · https://encodeai.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/SB-53-Coalition-Letter-9_24_2025.pdf"},{"category":"transparency-reports","label":"Transparency reports (incl. pre-deployment catastrophic-risk assessments)","definition":"The developer must publish a report at deployment describing how it assessed catastrophic risk, what it found, and how third parties were involved. The California Report treated pre-deployment assessments as early indicators of how models may affect society, and SB 53 accomplishes this with a requirement that large developers publish transparency reports that include the results of their pre-deployment assessments of catastrophic risk.","strongest_state":null,"provision_cite":null,"frontier_note":"CONVERGED, not empty. On the enacted text, California and New York both require summaries of the developer's assessment of catastrophic risk, the results of that assessment, and the extent to which third-party evaluators were involved — materially the same standard. An earlier draft of this map read New York's requirement as going beyond California here; that reading did not survive a full read of the enacted § 1421(3)(b) and has been corrected.","rationale":"On the enacted text, CA and NY require materially the same summary standard; recorded as converged.","confidence":"medium","cell_type":"converged","definition_source":"Encode pillar language, adapted: Encode AI et al., SB 53 coalition letter to Gov. Newsom (23 signatory organizations), 2025-09-24 · https://encodeai.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/SB-53-Coalition-Letter-9_24_2025.pdf"},{"category":"incident-reporting","label":"Critical incident reporting","definition":"The developer must report critical safety incidents to a state authority within a fixed window. The California Report concluded that an adverse event reporting system combining mandatory developer reporting with voluntary user reporting maximally grows the evidence base, and SB 53 takes exactly this approach by establishing a tightly defined set of critical safety incidents that AI developers are required to report. Under the enacted statute those reports go to the Office of Emergency Services, which must also establish a mechanism for the public to report critical safety incidents.","strongest_state":"IL","provision_cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 15(c) (72 hours to the designated Agency and the Attorney General; 24 hours where death or serious injury is imminent)","rationale":"Illinois and New York both run a 72-hour clock against California's 15 days. Illinois edges New York by requiring the 72-hour report to go to both the designated agency and the Attorney General, so the enforcing authority learns of the incident directly rather than secondhand. In the 24-hour imminent-risk case, all three states route the report to an authority with jurisdiction (as in California), not only to the primary regulator.","confidence":"high","definition_source":"Encode pillar language, adapted — its clause naming the Attorney General as recipient is NOT used, because the enacted statute routes California incident reports to the Office of Emergency Services: Encode AI et al., SB 53 coalition letter to Gov. Newsom (23 signatory organizations), 2025-09-24 · https://encodeai.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/SB-53-Coalition-Letter-9_24_2025.pdf — recipient per Justine Gluck, Future of Privacy Forum, 2025-10-03 · https://fpf.org/blog/californias-sb-53-the-first-frontier-ai-law-explained/"},{"category":"whistleblower","label":"Whistleblower protection","definition":"Employees responsible for risk assessment are protected when they disclose evidence of catastrophic risk, and cannot be contractually silenced. The California Report recommended strengthening whistleblower protections, pointing out that actions that may clearly pose a risk and violate company policies may not violate any existing laws. That recommendation is mirrored in SB 53, which allows employees to report evidence of catastrophic risks as well as violations of SB 53 itself to government authorities with legal protections against retaliation.","strongest_state":"CA","provision_cite":"Cal. Lab. Code § 1107.1","rationale":"California protects disclosure of evidence of catastrophic-risk danger even where no law has been broken — the protection is keyed to the danger, not to a legal violation, which is the case that matters most for frontier risk. Illinois adds better procedural machinery (anonymous channel, monthly updates to the reporter, AG hotline) but on the substantive trigger California is still the high-water mark. New York has no whistleblower provisions at all.","confidence":"medium","definition_source":"Encode pillar language, adapted: Encode AI et al., SB 53 coalition letter to Gov. Newsom (23 signatory organizations), 2025-09-24 · https://encodeai.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/SB-53-Coalition-Letter-9_24_2025.pdf"},{"category":"independent-verification","label":"Independent verification (annual third-party audits)","definition":"An independent auditor, not the developer, verifies compliance with the safety framework, and a summary is made public. Illinois builds on the frameworks California and New York have already passed and goes one step further by requiring independent audits, so the public doesn't have to take AI companies at their word.","strongest_state":"IL","provision_cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 10(d) (annual independent audit; public high-level summary within 30 days per Sec. 10(d)(4)(A); audits begin 2028-01-01, or 90 days after a developer first qualifies, whichever is later)","rationale":"Illinois is the first state to require annual independent third-party audits of frontier-AI safety compliance. California requires disclosure of whether third parties were involved in assessment; it does not require an audit. (Note: an unconfirmed secondary source suggests New York may have a one-time audit-at-qualification mechanism, so the claim here is scoped to the annual requirement.) The counter-case is worth holding while reading this cell: an independent verifier that depends on the developers it clears for repeat business has a significant incentive to grade gently, and a developer shopping among verifiers will find the one that does. Competition, the feature that is meant to make the model effective, instead drives it toward laxity. — Gabriel Weil","confidence":"high","definition_source":"Sunny Gandhi (Encode AI), quoted in Office of Gov. JB Pritzker, signing release, 2026-07-06 · https://gov-pritzker-newsroom.prezly.com/gov-pritzker-signs-nation-leading-artificial-intelligence-safety-law — counterpoint from Gabriel Weil, AI Frontiers, 2026-07-29 · https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/dont-let-ai-developers-hire-their-own-referees"},{"category":"enforcement","label":"Enforcement teeth","definition":"Who can sue, and for how much. Under SB 53 private actors do not have standing to sue a large developer for breaking transparency rules. Only the California AG can bring suit. The same holds in New York and Illinois.","strongest_state":"IL","provision_cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 25 (civil penalty does not exceed $1,000,000 for a first violation, $3,000,000 for subsequent violations)","rationale":"Illinois and New York both cap penalties at up to $1M/$3M against California's cap of up to $1M per violation. Illinois edges New York by pairing the penalty with the audit requirement, so noncompliance is more likely to be detected in the first place. Enforcement is AG-only in all three frontier states — no private right of action anywhere. A penalty nobody can trigger is not teeth.","confidence":"high","definition_source":"Kodama annotation to § 22757.15(b): Miles Kodama (AI Futures Project), sb53.info · https://sb53.info/#annotation_4b59813a"},{"category":"thresholds-scoping","label":"Scope: thresholds and catastrophic-risk definitions","definition":"Which developers and which models are covered — the compute floor, the revenue floor, and the definition of the harm being regulated. Recent state frontier AI laws share definitions of large frontier developer, frontier model (encompassing foundation models trained using more than 10^26 computational operations), and catastrophic risk (covering certain risks of death, serious injury, or major property damage arising from frontier models). In California and New York, catastrophic risk means a foreseeable and material risk that a frontier model could contribute to the death or serious injury of 50 or more people or cause at least $1 billion in damages. The same definition also reaches a model that could provide expert-level assistance in creating or releasing a chemical, biological, radiological, or nuclear weapon; engage in criminal conduct or a cyberattack without meaningful human intervention; or evade the control of its developer or user.","strongest_state":null,"provision_cite":null,"frontier_note":"CONVERGED, not empty. All three frontier states landed on the same pair of numbers — 10^26 FLOP for the model, $500M annual revenue for the developer — so there is no leader to name. New York opened with a tougher test keyed to $100M in aggregate training compute cost and traded it away in negotiation. Convergence is efficient for compliance and fragile for safety: if 10^26 is the wrong number, every frontier state is wrong at once, and a single federal preemption fight would be aimed at a single target.","rationale":"Recorded as a converged cell, not a contest. The interesting fact here is the regression, not the ranking.","confidence":"medium","cell_type":"converged","definition_source":"Justine Gluck, Future of Privacy Forum, 2026-06-09 · https://fpf.org/blog/frontier-ai-goes-federal-how-the-great-american-ai-act-compares-to-state-laws/ — four-limb catastrophic-risk list per Justine Gluck, Future of Privacy Forum, 2026-01-08 · https://fpf.org/blog/the-raise-act-vs-sb-53-a-tale-of-two-frontier-ai-laws/"},{"category":"internal-use-risk","label":"Internal-use risk","definition":"Whether the law covers catastrophic risk arising from a developer's own INTERNAL use of frontier models, not only from public deployment. Even before deployment, a model is vulnerable to theft by malicious outside actors and to catastrophic misuse by the developer's own staff. These internal models are the first to develop dual-use capabilities in areas like cyberoffense and bioweapon design, and as AI capabilities accelerate, the gap between internal and public capabilities could widen — some important systems may never be publicly released at all.","strongest_state":"CA","provision_cite":"Cal. B&P Code §22757.12(d); NY GBS §1421(1)(d); IL SB 315 Sec. 10(a)","rationale":"California is strongest because its internal-use duty runs on a clock rather than on a release: § 22757.12(d) requires a large frontier developer to summarize its assessment of catastrophic risk from its own internal use of frontier models and transmit that summary to the Office of Emergency Services every three months. That is the only standing reporting duty to a state office anywhere on this board (the statute allows another reasonable schedule the developer specifies). New York reaches internal use through the framework instead: § 1421(1)(d) requires the framework to cover the decision 'to deploy a frontier model or use it extensively internally'. Illinois Sec. 10(a) likewise lists internal use among the framework's required topics. All three facts are primary-verified. For contrast, the EU's Code of Practice does not include internally deployed models: the AI Act only regulates models placed on the EU market, and models trained and used exclusively for internal AI research and development remain outside the Code's scope. That is a crucial gap for loss-of-control scenarios, where internally deployed models are a significant source of risk.","confidence":"medium","definition_source":"Kodama annotation to § 22757.12(a)(10): Miles Kodama (AI Futures Project), sb53.info · https://sb53.info/#annotation_2d629045 — internal-capability argument from Oscar Delaney and Ashwin Acharya, AI Frontiers, 2025-08-28 · https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/the-hidden-ai-frontier — EU contrast from Henry Papadatos, AI Frontiers, 2025-07-12 · https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/how-the-eus-code-of-practice-advances-ai-safety"},{"category":"minimum-bar-standards","label":"Substantive minimum-bar standards (incl. deployment restraint)","definition":"Whether the law holds safety practices to a substantive quality bar, or bars deploying a model that poses an unreasonable risk of catastrophic harm — a duty to be good enough, or a duty not to ship, as distinct from a duty to disclose. There is currently relatively little organized political appetite for — and strong industry pressures against — traditional command-and-control regulatory measures such as mandates, prohibitions, and prescriptive safety requirements. That is the political fact this empty cell records.","strongest_state":null,"provision_cite":null,"frontier_note":"EMPTY — and it was not always. New York's original RAISE Act contained a flat prohibition: 'A large developer shall not deploy a frontier model if doing so would create an unreasonable risk of critical harm.' All ten sections of the enacted article were read in full to confirm what happened to it: § 1420 Definitions, § 1421 Transparency, § 1422 Reporting, § 1423 Loss of equity, § 1424 Duties, § 1425 Scope, § 1426 Exceptions, § 1427 Violations, § 1428 Disclosure, § 1429 Rulemaking. The prohibition does not appear anywhere in the enacted text — a full-text search returns zero occurrences of 'unreasonable risk', 'critical harm', or 'shall not deploy'. § 1427's violation list is exhaustive and does not include deploying a dangerous model as such. Nor does any state attach liability to catastrophic harm a model actually causes: that was the core of California SB 1047, vetoed in 2024, and every successor bill in every state dropped it. There is no penalty attached to deploying a dangerous model as such, in any state, and the penalties that do exist ($1M/$3M) attach to paperwork failures.","rationale":"Every enacted frontier AI law in the United States is disclosure-and-process: it governs what a developer must write down, publish, and report, not how good its safety practices must actually be. None bars deploying a dangerous model either — New York enacted such a bar and repealed it before it took effect. While the laws require AI companies to publish more information about their safety practices, there's nothing requiring those safety practices to meet any minimum bar for quality. And there is no way, in any of these laws, for anyone to say what you're doing is not enough, or you're doing something dangerous that needs to stop. — Thomas Woodside, Secure AI Project, in Transformer","confidence":"high","definition_source":"Yonathan Arbel, AI Frontiers, 2026-04-20 · https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/catalytic-regulation-incentivizing-safety-during-a-regulatory-drought — Woodside lines from Thomas Woodside (Secure AI Project), quoted in Transformer, 2026-07-28 · https://www.transformernews.ai/p/openai-hack-reveals-internal-deployment-risk"}],"states":[{"state":"AK","state_name":"Alaska","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"AL","state_name":"Alabama","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"AR","state_name":"Arkansas","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"AZ","state_name":"Arizona","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"CA","state_name":"California","status":"frontier-law","headline":"The baseline. Every frontier AI law in the country is measured against this one: SB 53 makes California the first state to enact a statute specifically targeting frontier artificial intelligence (AI) safety and transparency.","bills":[{"id":"SB 53","name":"Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act (TFAIA)","signed_date":"2025-09-29","effective":"2026-01-01 (in force)","status":"In force","text_url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53","explainers":[{"url":"https://sb53.info/","title":"California SB 53, annotated","publisher":"Miles Kodama (AI Futures Project)","date":"2025-10-25","annotation":"Full statutory text with plain-English annotations sitting directly beneath each clause, by an independent AI policy researcher; use it when you need the operative language rather than someone's summary of it."},{"url":"https://sb53.info/summary","title":"SB 53 in brief","publisher":"Miles Kodama (AI Futures Project)","date":"2025-10-25","annotation":"Seven bullets, each ending in a statutory cite — the fastest honest orientation to what the law actually requires."},{"url":"https://fpf.org/blog/californias-sb-53-the-first-frontier-ai-law-explained/","title":"California's SB 53: The First Frontier AI Law, Explained","publisher":"Future of Privacy Forum","date":"2025-10","annotation":"FPF's section-by-section walkthrough of the framework, transparency report, incident-reporting and whistleblower provisions; the reference most other explainers cite."},{"url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/emissary/2025/10/california-sb-53-frontier-ai-law-what-it-does","title":"California Just Passed the First U.S. Frontier AI Law. Here's What It Does.","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace","date":"2025-10-16","annotation":"Scott Singer and Alasdair Phillips-Robins on SB 53's trust-but-verify design and why it became the national template, from a think tank focused on AI geopolitics."},{"url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/what-is-californias-ai-safety-law/","title":"What is California's AI safety law?","publisher":"Brookings Institution","date":"unknown","annotation":"Frames SB 53 as an attempt to convert voluntary lab commitments into enforceable public accountability, and lays out the open questions that leaves."},{"url":"https://ai-analytics.wharton.upenn.edu/wharton-accountable-ai-lab/sb-53-what-californias-new-ai-safety-law-means-for-developers/","title":"What California's New AI Safety Law Means for Developers","publisher":"Wharton Accountable AI Lab","date":"2025-11-14","annotation":"Developer-facing breakdown separating the duties on all frontier developers from the extra duties on large ones, and the ex ante versus ex post regulatory choice underneath."}],"provisions":[{"summary":"Every large frontier developer must write, implement, comply with, and publicly publish a frontier AI framework covering ten enumerated topics — the statutory analogue of a frontier safety policy.","tag":"baseline","category":"frontier-ai-framework","cite":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.12(a)","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"On every deployment of a new frontier model, a large frontier developer must publish a transparency report explaining how it assessed catastrophic risk, what it found, and how third parties were involved.","tag":"baseline","category":"transparency-reports","cite":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.12(c)","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Every three months — or on another reasonable schedule it specifies — a large frontier developer must assess catastrophic risk arising from its own INTERNAL use of frontier models and send that assessment to the Office of Emergency Services. This is the one genuinely novel disclosure in SB 53 — industry practice had focused on external deployment risk, tied to releases rather than to a clock.","tag":"baseline","category":"internal-use-risk","cite":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.12(d)","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"The Office of Emergency Services operates a critical-incident reporting system. Frontier developers report critical safety incidents within 15 days, cut to 24 hours where the incident poses an imminent risk of death or serious physical injury. In that 24-hour case, the report goes to an authority — including any law enforcement agency or public safety agency with jurisdiction — not only to the Office.","tag":"baseline","category":"incident-reporting","cite":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.13; recipient for the 24-hour report per § 22757.13(c)(2)","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"SB 53 strengthens whistleblower protection for employees responsible for risk assessment or management. Critically, it protects disclosure of evidence that the developer's activities pose a catastrophic risk EVEN WHERE NO LAW HAS BEEN VIOLATED — existing Labor Code § 1102.5 protection is keyed to reporting a legal violation, and frontier danger need not involve one.","tag":"baseline","category":"whistleblower","cite":"Cal. Lab. Code § 1107.1","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Covered developers are those training models above 10^26 FLOP; the heavier obligations attach only to 'large' frontier developers with more than $500M in annual revenue.","tag":"baseline","category":"thresholds-scoping","cite":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.11","source":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Civil penalty of up to $1,000,000 per violation, enforceable by the Attorney General only. No private right of action.","tag":"baseline","category":"enforcement","cite":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.15","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"SB 53 requires a large frontier developer to DISCLOSE whether and how third parties were involved in its risk assessment. It does not require an independent audit, and no auditor independence standard attaches. This is the gap Illinois later closed.","tag":"baseline","category":"independent-verification","cite":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.12(c)","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"SB 53 attaches no liability to catastrophic harm actually caused by a model. Liability was the core of the vetoed SB 1047; the enacted TFAIA is a disclosure statute, and its penalties attach to failures to publish, report, or follow a self-authored framework.","tag":"baseline","category":"minimum-bar-standards","cite":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 22757.15","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"CalCompute: a consortium tasked with exploring a public AI compute cluster to support socially beneficial research. An industrial-policy provision rather than a safety obligation, and conditionally operative upon a future budget appropriation — it is not yet funded.","tag":"orthogonal","category":null,"cite":"Cal. Gov. Code § 11546.8","source":"https://sb53.info/summary","confidence":"high"}]}],"headline_source":"Justine Gluck, Future of Privacy Forum, 2025-10-03 · https://fpf.org/blog/californias-sb-53-the-first-frontier-ai-law-explained/ — statutory facts verified against the enrolled text at https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53"},{"state":"CO","state_name":"Colorado","status":"orthogonal","headline":"Colorado passed the first comprehensive state AI act in the country (2024). Enforcement was stayed in litigation, and the Act was repealed and reenacted on 2026-05-14 as the Automated Decision-Making Technology law, which eliminates the 2024 Act's governance requirements and references to algorithmic discrimination, focusing instead on transparency. Its current law regulates AI use in consequential decisions — covering both developers and deployers of such systems — not frontier model development.","bills":[{"id":"SB 24-205","name":"Colorado Artificial Intelligence Act (REPEALED)","signed_date":"2024-05-17","effective":"Never took effect","status":"Repealed — enforcement stayed 2026-04-27, repealed and reenacted 2026-05-14","text_url":"https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb24-205","explainers":[{"url":"https://www.mcdermottlaw.com/insights/colorado-ai-law-in-flux-comprehensive-replacement-bill-signed-after-federal-court-blocks-predecessor-s-enforcement/","title":"Colorado AI law in flux: replacement bill signed after federal court blocks predecessor's enforcement","publisher":"McDermott Will & Schulte","date":"2026-05","annotation":"Ties both threads together: the 2026-04-27 order blocking enforcement after xAI sued and DOJ intervened, and the 2026-05-14 signing that repealed and reenacted the Act."},{"url":"https://www.kelleydrye.com/viewpoints/blogs/ad-law-access/ai-regulatory-roundup-recent-developments-in-colorado-connecticut-and-california","title":"AI Regulatory Roundup: Colorado, Connecticut, and California","publisher":"Kelley Drye & Warren","date":"2026-05","annotation":"The most precise account of the stay's mechanics, quoting the order barring enforcement of the Act 'or any legislation replacing or amending' it.","date_warning":"Published before the 2026-05-14 signing; treats the replacement bill as pending."}],"provisions":[{"summary":"Imposed a duty of reasonable care on developers and deployers of high-risk AI systems to protect against algorithmic discrimination. The U.S. Department of Justice intervened in the pending suit against the Act, xAI v. Weiser, No. 1:26-cv-01515 (D. Colo., filed 2026-04-09), filing its Complaint in Intervention on 2026-04-24; a joint stay of enforcement was granted 2026-04-27. The duty of care was then removed entirely by the repeal-and-reenactment.","tag":"orthogonal","category":null,"cite":"Colo. SB 24-205 (repealed)","source":"Court docket: xAI v. Weiser, No. 1:26-cv-01515 (D. Colo., filed 2026-04-09); DOJ Complaint in Intervention, filed 2026-04-24 (justice.gov). Corroborating: https://www.mcdermottlaw.com/insights/colorado-ai-law-in-flux-comprehensive-replacement-bill-signed-after-federal-court-blocks-predecessor-s-enforcement/ and https://www.kelleydrye.com/viewpoints/blogs/ad-law-access/ai-regulatory-roundup-recent-developments-in-colorado-connecticut-and-california","confidence":"high"}]},{"id":"SB 26-189","name":"Automated Decision-Making Technology (replacement; the enrolled act has no short title)","signed_date":"2026-05-14","effective":"2027-01-01; applies to consequential decisions made on or after that date; Attorney General rulemaking pending","status":"Enacted, not yet effective","text_url":"https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189","explainers":[{"url":"https://natlawreview.com/article/colorados-sb-26-189-understanding-new-automated-decision-making-technology-law","title":"Understanding Colorado's New Automated Decision-Making Technology Law","publisher":"National Law Review","date":"2026-07-09","annotation":"The most current of the set: recaps xAI's constitutional theories and lays out the replacement framework's shift from algorithmic-discrimination duties to notice, explanation and human-review rights."},{"url":"https://www.nortonrosefulbright.com/en-us/knowledge/publications/18733d31/colorado-enacts-revised-ai-law","title":"Colorado enacts revised AI law","publisher":"Norton Rose Fulbright","date":"2026-05","annotation":"Compact corroborating summary of the repeal-and-replace, with back-links to the firm's coverage of the underlying litigation."}],"provisions":[{"summary":"A narrower automated-decision-making technology law replacing SB 24-205, codified as a repeal-and-reenactment of part 17, article 1, title 6. It regulates AI use in consequential decisions — imposing duties on both developers and deployers of automated decision-making technology, including technical-documentation duties running from developers to deployers, not developer duties alone. There is no compute threshold, no frontier model class, and no catastrophic-risk concept.","tag":"orthogonal","category":null,"cite":"Colo. SB 26-189; C.R.S. §§ 6-1-1701 et seq.","source":"https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189 (bill page verified live) and https://natlawreview.com/article/colorados-sb-26-189-understanding-new-automated-decision-making-technology-law","confidence":"high"}]}],"headline_source":"Tatiana Rice, Future of Privacy Forum, 2026-05-19 · https://fpf.org/blog/colorado-revises-its-ai-act-what-changed-and-why/ — signing date taken from the enrolled act (2026-05-14), which FPF's post gives one day later"},{"state":"CT","state_name":"Connecticut","status":"partial","headline":"SB 5 borrows the language of frontier AI laws, but not the full architecture. Like California's SB 53 and New York's RAISE Act, it defines key terms such as frontier developer, large frontier developer, and catastrophic risk. But unlike broader frontier AI frameworks, SB 5 does not require developers to publish governance frameworks, issue transparency reports, or establish critical safety incident reporting mechanisms. Instead, it focuses solely on protecting employees who report certain serious AI-related risks.","bills":[{"id":"Public Act 26-15 (SB 5)","name":"Connecticut Artificial Intelligence Responsibility and Transparency Act (CART); official statutory title: An Act Concerning Online Safety","signed_date":"2026-05-27","effective":"Frontier provisions (Sec. 2) 2026-10-01","status":"Enacted","text_url":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/act/pa/pdf/2026PA-00015-R00SB-00005-PA.pdf","explainers":[{"url":"https://www.ropesgray.com/en/insights/alerts/2026/06/connecticut-enacts-sweeping-ai-law-covering-employment-healthcare-and-online-safety","title":"Connecticut Enacts Sweeping AI Law Covering Employment, Healthcare, and Online Safety","publisher":"Ropes & Gray LLP","date":"2026-06-03","annotation":"The most thorough CART alert, covering all five regulated surfaces with pin cites; the frontier-developer piece is a small part of a much broader statute."},{"url":"https://ktslaw.com/insights/alert/2026/6/connecticut-enacts-new-ai-legislation-what-businesses-need-to-know-about-sb-5","title":"What Businesses Need to Know About SB 5","publisher":"Kilpatrick Townsend & Stockton","date":"2026-06","annotation":"Cleanest compliance-calendar treatment of the frontier-developer provisions: anti-retaliation from 2026-10-01, anonymous internal reporting channels by 2027-01-01."}],"provisions":[{"summary":"Anti-retaliation duties protecting employees of large developers who raise safety concerns, plus a required anonymous internal reporting channel. This is the entire frontier AI content of the Act.","tag":"weaker","category":"whistleblower","cite":"Conn. Pub. Act 26-15, Sec. 2","source":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/act/pa/pdf/2026PA-00015-R00SB-00005-PA.pdf","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"No safety framework requirement, no evaluation requirement, no public transparency reports. Connecticut adopted the reporting channel without the thing to report about.","tag":"weaker","category":"frontier-ai-framework","cite":"Conn. Pub. Act 26-15 (by absence)","source":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/act/pa/pdf/2026PA-00015-R00SB-00005-PA.pdf","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"Civil penalty of up to $1,000 per violation, enforceable by the Attorney General only.","tag":"weaker","category":"enforcement","cite":"Conn. Pub. Act 26-15, Sec. 2","source":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/2026/act/pa/pdf/2026PA-00015-R00SB-00005-PA.pdf","confidence":"medium"}],"bill_status_url":"https://www.cga.ct.gov/asp/cgabillstatus/cgabillstatus.asp?selBillType=Bill&bill_num=SB05&which_year=2026","signed_date_note":"RESOLVED: signed 2026-05-27 per the CGA bill-status page. The 2026-06-02 date circulating in several law-firm alerts is the governor's public-announcement date, not the signing date.","warning":"If you see this Act dated 2026-06-02, that source is citing the governor's announcement, not the signing. The Public Act number is unambiguous either way."}],"headline_source":"Justine Gluck and Daniel Hales, Future of Privacy Forum, 2026-05-27 · https://fpf.org/blog/sb-5-in-five-what-to-know-about-connecticuts-new-ai-law/ — signing date primary-verified against the Connecticut General Assembly bill-status page"},{"state":"DC","state_name":"District of Columbia","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"DE","state_name":"Delaware","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"FL","state_name":"Florida","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"GA","state_name":"Georgia","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"HI","state_name":"Hawaii","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"IA","state_name":"Iowa","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"ID","state_name":"Idaho","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"IL","state_name":"Illinois","status":"frontier-law","headline":"On the enacted text, Illinois goes furthest: annual independent audits of compliance, with a public high-level summary, beginning 2028-01-01. Illinois is the first state to mandate independent third-party evaluations of AI safety practices, making its AI law the strongest in the country. — Nick Beckstead, Secure AI Project","bills":[{"id":"SB 315 (enrolled as Public Act 104-0538)","name":"Artificial Intelligence Safety Measures Act","signed_date":"2026-07-06","effective":"2027-01-01; framework and audit obligations begin 2028-01-01, or 90 days after a developer first qualifies as a large frontier developer, whichever is later","status":"Enacted, not yet effective","text_url":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","note":"Section cites below are to the enrolled Public Act 104-0538, verified section-by-section: Sec. 5 (definitions), Sec. 10(d) (audits), Sec. 15(c) (incident reporting), Sec. 17(a)(4) (federal safe harbor), Sec. 25 (penalties), Sec. 35 (home-rule preemption). Provisions without a section pin below have not been verified to that level and are cited to the Act generally.","explainers":[{"url":"https://gov-pritzker-newsroom.prezly.com/gov-pritzker-signs-nation-leading-artificial-intelligence-safety-law","title":"Gov. Pritzker Signs Nation-Leading Artificial Intelligence Safety Law","publisher":"Office of Gov. JB Pritzker (official)","date":"2026-07-06","annotation":"The signing-day release, useful for the state's own framing and for on-record supportive quotes from Anthropic, OpenAI, Encode, the Secure AI Project and the Transparency Coalition."},{"url":"https://capitolnewsillinois.com/news/pritzker-signs-landmark-ai-regulation-bill-that-aims-to-mitigate-risks/","title":"Pritzker signs landmark AI regulation bill that aims to mitigate risks","publisher":"Capitol News Illinois (nonprofit newsroom, funded by the Illinois Press Foundation and the McCormick Foundation)","date":"2026-07-06","annotation":"Independent statehouse reporting from the signing, and the clearest short statement that SB 315 mirrors SB 53 and RAISE while adding the audit requirement."},{"url":"https://www.mcdonaldhopkins.com/insights/news/illinois-artificial-intelligence-safety-measures-act","title":"Big tech, meet big oversight: An overview of Illinois' AI Safety Measures Act","publisher":"McDonald Hopkins LLC","date":"2026-07","annotation":"Pins down the covered-entity test ($500M revenue plus more than 10^26 operations) and itemizes the framework, third-party evaluation and whistleblower obligations."},{"url":"https://www.wired.com/story/illinois-pass-major-ai-safety-law-pritzker/","title":"Illinois Lawmakers Just Passed America's Strongest AI Safety Bill","publisher":"WIRED","date":"2026-05-27","annotation":"National reporting you will not find in the legal alerts, including the sponsor's own account and the expectation that Big Four firms would perform the mandated audits.","date_warning":"Published at House passage, roughly six weeks before signing; describes the bill as heading to the governor's desk."}],"provisions":[{"summary":"The first state to require annual independent third-party audits of compliance with the published safety framework. The auditor must have no mutual financial interest with the developer and payment cannot be conditioned on the audit's results; a public high-level summary is due within 30 days.","tag":"frontier-advancing","category":"independent-verification","cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 10(d) (audit obligations effective 2028-01-01; public summary per Sec. 10(d)(4)(A))","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Enhanced whistleblower machinery: an anonymous internal reporting channel, monthly status updates to the person who reported, quarterly summaries to corporate officers, and a direct Attorney General hotline. Better process than California, which still holds the broader substantive trigger.","tag":"frontier-advancing","category":"whistleblower","cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"Critical incident reporting within 72 hours to both the designated Agency and the Attorney General, cut to 24 hours — to an authority with jurisdiction (as in California) — where harm is imminent. Against California's 15 days.","tag":"frontier-advancing","category":"incident-reporting","cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 15(c)","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Civil penalties of up to $1,000,000 for a first violation and up to $3,000,000 for subsequent violations, Attorney General only, no private right of action.","tag":"frontier-advancing","category":"enforcement","cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 25","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Tightened federal safe harbor: a federal regime only displaces the Illinois obligations if that federal regime itself includes third-party audits. Illinois refuses to trade its strongest provision away for federal cover.","tag":"frontier-advancing","category":"enforcement","cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 17(a)(4)","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Large developers must publish a frontier AI framework on the same ten-topic template used by California and New York.","tag":"copies-SB53","category":"frontier-ai-framework","cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538 (effective 2028-01-01, or 90 days after a developer first qualifies, whichever is later)","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"Thresholds copied from the converged template: 10^26 FLOP and $500M revenue, with catastrophic risk as the regulated harm.","tag":"copies-SB53","category":"thresholds-scoping","cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 5","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"No deployment prohibition and no liability for harm caused. Like California and post-amendment New York, Illinois regulates disclosure and process, not the decision to ship.","tag":"copies-SB53","category":"minimum-bar-standards","cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538 (by absence)","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"Preempts home-rule localities, so Illinois municipalities cannot legislate their own frontier-AI rules. A federalism provision with no SB 53 analogue, and not a safety axis in either direction.","tag":"orthogonal","category":null,"cite":"IL Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 35","source":"https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocNum=315&GAID=18&DocTypeID=SB&SessionID=114&GA=104","confidence":"high"}]}],"headline_source":"Nick Beckstead (Secure AI Project), quoted in Office of Gov. JB Pritzker, signing release, 2026-07-06 · https://gov-pritzker-newsroom.prezly.com/gov-pritzker-signs-nation-leading-artificial-intelligence-safety-law — audit mechanics primary-verified against Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 10(d)"},{"state":"IN","state_name":"Indiana","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"KS","state_name":"Kansas","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"KY","state_name":"Kentucky","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"LA","state_name":"Louisiana","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"MA","state_name":"Massachusetts","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"MD","state_name":"Maryland","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"ME","state_name":"Maine","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"MI","state_name":"Michigan","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"MN","state_name":"Minnesota","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"MO","state_name":"Missouri","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"MS","state_name":"Mississippi","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"MT","state_name":"Montana","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"NC","state_name":"North Carolina","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"ND","state_name":"North Dakota","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"NE","state_name":"Nebraska","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"NH","state_name":"New Hampshire","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"NJ","state_name":"New Jersey","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"NM","state_name":"New Mexico","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"NV","state_name":"Nevada","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"NY","state_name":"New York","status":"frontier-law","headline":"New York passed the strongest frontier AI law in the country, then amended it before it took effect: penalties cut from $10M/$30M to $1M/$3M and the deployment prohibition removed. The surviving text still goes beyond California on incident reporting and developer registration. Perhaps the most significant change was the removal of the deployment prohibition; penalties were similarly scaled back, falling from a maximum of $10 million for a first violation and $30 million for subsequent violations to $1 million and $3 million, respectively.","bills":[{"id":"RAISE Act (S6953-B / A6453-B), as amended by S8828","name":"Responsible AI Safety and Education Act","signed_date":"2025-12-19","amended_date":"2026-03-27","effective":"2027-01-01","status":"Enacted, not yet effective — READ THE AMENDED TEXT","text_url":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","amendment_url":"https://legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/bills/2025/S8828","original_text_url":"https://legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/bills/2025/S6953B","warning":"Any comparison chart dated before March 2026 describes a RAISE Act that is not the law. S8828 did not tweak the statute — it repealed and replaced the whole of General Business Law Article 44-B. The Laws Affected line reads 'Rpld & add Art 44-B §§1420 - 1429, Gen Bus L'.","explainers":[{"url":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","title":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 1421, as enacted","publisher":"New York State Senate (primary source)","date":"2026-03-27","annotation":"The consolidated text of § 1421 as it now stands, titled Transparency requirements — read this rather than any summary, because most published summaries describe the superseded version."},{"url":"https://legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/bills/2025/S8828","title":"S8828, the chapter amendment","publisher":"New York State Senate (primary source)","date":"2026-03-27","annotation":"The enacted amendment text as PDF. The bill-status page at nysenate.gov carries the Laws Affected line reading 'Rpld & add Art 44-B §§1420-1429, Gen Bus L', which is what proves this was a repeal-and-replace rather than an in-place edit."},{"url":"https://legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/bills/2025/S6953B","title":"The original RAISE Act (S6953-B)","publisher":"New York State Senate (primary source)","date":"2025-12-19","annotation":"The December 2025 text, preserved because the diff between it and the current law — especially the deleted deployment prohibition — is the most important fact about New York."},{"url":"https://www.mofo.com/resources/insights/260403-new-york-amends-the-raise-act-to-align-more-closely","title":"New York Amends the RAISE Act to Align More Closely with California Law","publisher":"Morrison Foerster","date":"2026-04-03","annotation":"POST-AMENDMENT and the best secondary source for New York: enumerates exactly what S8828 changed, including the threshold swap and the penalty cut from $10M/$30M to $1M/$3M."},{"url":"https://iapp.org/news/a/the-new-frontier-lessons-from-california-s-sb-53-and-new-york-s-raise-act","title":"The New Frontier: Lessons from California's SB 53 and New York's RAISE Act","publisher":"IAPP","date":"2026-04-29","annotation":"POST-AMENDMENT: reads the amendment as a case of the Sacramento Effect, with California's design propagating outward. Partly member-gated."},{"url":"https://carnegieendowment.org/emissary/2026/02/ai-state-law-new-york-raise-act-california-sb53","title":"With the RAISE Act, New York Aligns With California on Frontier AI Laws","publisher":"Carnegie Endowment for International Peace","date":"2026-02-05","annotation":"Source of the widely quoted line that RAISE 'in many cases directly copies the text of the California law', and still the best conceptual read on convergence.","date_warning":"PRE-AMENDMENT. Published before S8828 was signed on 2026-03-27; its threshold and penalty details describe a law that no longer exists."},{"url":"https://fpf.org/blog/the-raise-act-vs-sb-53-a-tale-of-two-frontier-ai-laws/","title":"The RAISE Act vs. SB 53: A Tale of Two Frontier AI Laws","publisher":"Future of Privacy Forum","date":"2025-12","annotation":"FPF's side-by-side of the two frontier laws; valuable framing, but it anticipates the conforming amendments as a future event.","date_warning":"PRE-AMENDMENT. The text explicitly expects the conforming changes to be adopted 'in January'. No updated FPF version was found. Prefer the MoFo alert above."}],"provisions":[{"summary":"REMOVED: the deployment prohibition. The original RAISE Act § 1421(2) read, verbatim: 'A large developer shall not deploy a frontier model if doing so would create an unreasonable risk of critical harm.' The S8828 amendment repealed and replaced the entire article, and the prohibition appears nowhere in the enacted text. § 1427 (Violations) now enumerates an exhaustive liability list keyed to failures to publish, transmit, report, or follow one's own framework; there is no penalty for deploying a dangerous model as such.","tag":"weaker","trajectory":"ratcheted-down","trajectory_note":"New York is the only state that has ever enacted a substantive duty not to ship. It repealed that duty before the law took effect. This is the single strongest piece of evidence that the state-level ratchet is not monotonic.","category":"minimum-bar-standards","cite":"compare original § 1421(2) with enacted §§ 1421, 1427","source":"https://legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/bills/2025/S6953B and https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","verification_note":"Method: all ten sections of the enacted article were read in full — § 1420 Definitions, § 1421 Transparency, § 1422 Reporting, § 1423 Loss of equity, § 1424 Duties, § 1425 Scope, § 1426 Exceptions, § 1427 Violations, § 1428 Disclosure, § 1429 Rulemaking — and a full-text search of §§ 1420–1429 returns zero occurrences of 'unreasonable risk', 'critical harm', or 'shall not deploy', so the prohibition did not migrate to another section.","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"REDUCED: civil penalties were cut from up to $10M for a first violation / up to $30M for subsequent violations, to up to $1M / up to $3M, realigning New York with California. Still above California in one respect: repeat violations cost more.","tag":"frontier-advancing","trajectory":"ratcheted-down","trajectory_note":"Beats California on repeat violations, but only after a 90% cut from what New York originally enacted. Scoring the final text alone reads this as New York leading; the trajectory reads the other way.","category":"enforcement","cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 1427","source":"https://legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/bills/2025/S8828","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"Large frontier developers must publish a frontier AI framework describing how they handle catastrophic risk, including how they review assessments and mitigation adequacy as part of the decision to deploy a model or use it extensively internally. Materially tracks California.","tag":"copies-SB53","category":"frontier-ai-framework","cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 1421(1)","source":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Enacted § 1421(3)(b) requires large frontier developers to publish summaries of their assessments of catastrophic risk, the results of those assessments, and the extent to which third-party evaluators were involved — materially the same standard as SB 53, not the stricter standard an earlier secondary source suggested.","tag":"copies-SB53","category":"transparency-reports","cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 1421(3)(b)","source":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Safety incidents must be reported within 72 hours, cut to 24 hours — to an authority with jurisdiction (as in California) — where death or serious injury is imminent. Against California's 15 days.","tag":"frontier-advancing","category":"incident-reporting","cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 1422(3)","source":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"NO WHISTLEBLOWER PROVISIONS AT ALL. The enacted Article 44-B (§§ 1420–1429) contains no whistleblower or anti-retaliation section. California and Illinois both have one; Connecticut's partial law is built almost entirely out of one.","tag":"weaker","category":"whistleblower","cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law art. 44-B, §§ 1420–1429 (by absence)","source":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"A Department of Financial Services registration and disclosure program for large frontier developers, with biennial renewal and penalties of up to $1,000 per day for noncompliance, plus DFS rulemaking authority. No other state stands up a dedicated regulator relationship.","tag":"frontier-advancing","category":"enforcement","cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law §§ 1428, 1429","source":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"Thresholds converged onto California's 10^26 FLOP and $500M revenue. The original RAISE Act used a tougher test keyed to models 'the compute cost of which exceeds one hundred million dollars' (original § 1420), which was traded away in the amendment negotiation.","tag":"copies-SB53","trajectory":"ratcheted-down","trajectory_note":"A cell that looks like harmless convergence in a final-text comparison is actually a concession. This is exactly the case that made a single-axis tag unusable.","category":"thresholds-scoping","cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 1420","source":"https://legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/bills/2025/S6953B (original text) and https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421 (enacted text)","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"The amendment swapped the operative vocabulary from 'large developer' and 'critical harm' to 'large frontier developer' and 'catastrophic risk', tracking California's terms of art. This is substantive convergence, not renaming — it means New York is no longer an independent regulatory regime with its own definitions.","tag":"copies-SB53","trajectory":"ratcheted-down","trajectory_note":"Definitional convergence is why the four frontier states can be compared on one axis at all — and why a single bad threshold would be a single point of failure nationwide.","category":"thresholds-scoping","cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 1420","source":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","confidence":"high"},{"summary":"Scope covers models developed, deployed, or operating in whole or in part in New York state. The statute also provides a third-party-causation defense: 'Nothing in this article shall be construed to prevent a large frontier developer from asserting that another person, entity, or factor, may be responsible for any alleged harm.'","tag":"weaker","category":null,"cite":"N.Y. Gen. Bus. Law § 1427(3)","source":"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/GBS/1421","confidence":"high"}]}],"headline_source":"Penalty and prohibition changes primary-verified against the enacted Art. 44-B text; corroborating description from Justine Gluck, Future of Privacy Forum, 2026-01-08 · https://fpf.org/blog/the-raise-act-vs-sb-53-a-tale-of-two-frontier-ai-laws/"},{"state":"OH","state_name":"Ohio","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"OK","state_name":"Oklahoma","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"OR","state_name":"Oregon","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"PA","state_name":"Pennsylvania","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"RI","state_name":"Rhode Island","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"SC","state_name":"South Carolina","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"SD","state_name":"South Dakota","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"TN","state_name":"Tennessee","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"TX","state_name":"Texas","status":"orthogonal","headline":"A comprehensive AI usage law: it restricts the development and deployment of AI systems for certain purposes, including violations of constitutional rights, unlawful discrimination, generating sexually explicit content, and encouraging criminal activity or physical harm. It also regulates government agencies' use of AI, establishes a 36-month regulatory sandbox for testing AI systems, and creates the Texas AI Advisory Council. It regulates how AI is used, not frontier development, so it is not ranked against SB 53.","bills":[{"id":"HB 149","name":"Texas Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act (TRAIGA)","signed_date":"2025-06-22","effective":"2026-01-01 (in force)","status":"In force","text_url":"https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=89R&Bill=HB149","explainers":[{"url":"https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/89R/analysis/html/HB00149S.htm","title":"Official Bill Analysis, C.S.H.B. 149","publisher":"Texas Legislative Council (official)","date":"2025-05-20","annotation":"The state's own section-by-section explanation of TRAIGA, including the prohibited practices, the regulatory sandbox and the AI Council — a primary-source alternative to any firm summary."},{"url":"https://www.bakerbotts.com/thought-leadership/publications/2025/july/texas-enacts-responsible-ai-governance-act-what-companies-need-to-know","title":"Texas Enacts Responsible AI Governance Act: What Companies Need to Know","publisher":"Baker Botts L.L.P.","date":"2025-07","annotation":"Explains TRAIGA's defining choice — intent-based liability for named practices rather than the impact-based EU-style regime of the original draft — which is exactly why it does not sit on the SB 53 axis."},{"url":"https://www.klgates.com/Pared-Back-Version-of-the-Texas-Responsible-Artificial-Intelligence-Governance-Act-Signed-Into-Law-6-24-2025","title":"Pared Back Version of TRAIGA Signed Into Law","publisher":"K&L Gates LLP","date":"2025-06-24","annotation":"Traces how HB 1709 was cut down into HB 149, itemizing which prohibitions survived and which private-sector obligations were stripped out."}],"provisions":[{"summary":"Intent-based prohibitions on specified harmful uses of AI, duties on government entities, and a safe harbor for developers following the NIST AI Risk Management Framework. Penalties run in curable and uncurable tiers from $10,000 to $200,000, plus additional per-day amounts for continuing violations.","tag":"orthogonal","category":null,"cite":"Tex. HB 149","source":"https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=89R&Bill=HB149","confidence":"medium"},{"summary":"TRAIGA has no frontier-developer class and no catastrophic-risk framework. It regulates intent and use; SB 53 regulates capability and disclosure. Comparing them on a single scale would produce a number that means nothing.","tag":"orthogonal","category":null,"cite":"Tex. HB 149 (by absence)","source":"https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=89R&Bill=HB149","confidence":"high"}]}],"headline_source":"Future of Privacy Forum, Enacted AI Legislation Chart, 2026-02-18 · https://fpf.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Enacted-AI-Legislation-Chart-.pdf (Texas entry; the chart's Colorado and SB 53 entries are stale and are not used)"},{"state":"UT","state_name":"Utah","status":"orthogonal","headline":"The first state AI disclosure law in the country (2024): it creates disclosure requirements for entities providing consumer-facing generative AI systems to individuals in regulated occupations, and creates a regulatory sandbox and the Office of AI Policy and AI Learning Laboratory Program. Two 2025 amendments narrowed and extended it — SB 226 limits the generative AI disclosure requirements to high-risk interactions, where a system collects sensitive personal information and involves significant decision-making, such as in financial, legal, medical, and mental health contexts; SB 332 extends the Act's expiration date by two years, so its provisions remain in effect until July 2027. An AI usage law, not a frontier-developer law.","bills":[{"id":"SB 149","name":"Artificial Intelligence Policy Act (as amended 2025)","signed_date":"2024-03-13","effective":"2024-05-01; sunsets 2027-07-01","status":"In force, sunsetting","text_url":"https://le.utah.gov/~2024/bills/static/SB0149.html","explainers":[{"url":"https://fpf.org/blog/chatbots-in-check-utahs-latest-ai-legislation/","title":"Chatbots in Check: Utah's Latest AI Legislation","publisher":"Future of Privacy Forum","date":"2025-04","annotation":"Explainer on the 2025 amendment package: SB 332 pushing the sunset to July 2027 and SB 226 narrowing the disclosure duty to consumer request or high-risk interactions."},{"url":"https://www.davispolk.com/insights/client-update/utah-scales-back-reach-generative-ai-consumer-protection-law","title":"Utah scales back reach of generative AI consumer protection law","publisher":"Davis Polk & Wardwell","date":"2025","annotation":"Concise account of how the 2025 bills refocused the Act on high-risk generative-AI interactions, added a safe harbor, and extended the repeal date."},{"url":"https://www.dwt.com/blogs/artificial-intelligence-law-advisor/2025/04/utah-regulation-ai-policy-mental-health-chatbots","title":"Utah Enacts Multiple Laws Expanding Its Regulation of AI","publisher":"Davis Wright Tremaine","date":"2025-04","annotation":"Covers the full 2025 Utah package rather than the Act alone, including the point that using a generative-AI tool is not an affirmative defense to a consumer-protection violation."}],"provisions":[{"summary":"Requires disclosure that a consumer is interacting with generative AI in certain regulated-occupation and consumer-transaction contexts, and establishes an Office of AI Policy. The 2025 amendments narrowed the disclosure trigger. The Act sunsets 2027-07-01.","tag":"orthogonal","category":null,"cite":"Utah SB 149","source":"https://le.utah.gov/~2024/bills/static/SB0149.html","confidence":"medium"}]}],"headline_source":"Future of Privacy Forum, Enacted AI Legislation Chart, 2026-02-18 · https://fpf.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Enacted-AI-Legislation-Chart-.pdf (Utah SB 149, SB 226 and SB 332 entries; the chart's Colorado and SB 53 entries are stale and are not used)"},{"state":"VA","state_name":"Virginia","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"VT","state_name":"Vermont","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"WA","state_name":"Washington","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"WI","state_name":"Wisconsin","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"WV","state_name":"West Virginia","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]},{"state":"WY","state_name":"Wyoming","status":"none","headline":"No frontier-model statute found. Many states regulate AI in other ways, outside this map's scope.","bills":[]}],"federal":{"headline":"No federal preemption is in force. Every obligation on this map is currently live law.","items":[{"summary":"The proposed 10-year federal moratorium on state AI enforcement was stripped from the 2025 reconciliation bill by a Senate vote of 99-1, and a preemption provision was declined again in the FY2026 NDAA. The proposed moratorium was a prohibition, not preemption — one for which there is little historical precedent. Traditional federal preemption replaces state laws with federal standards; here, the federal government would have knocked out virtually all state and local laws without a federal law to fill the void it created.","confidence":"low","source":"Vote count and NDAA outcome: internal note, public source pending. Prohibition-not-preemption analysis: Kristin O'Donoghue, AI Frontiers, 2025-06-25 · https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/congress-might-block-states-from-regulating-ai"},{"summary":"Executive Order 14365 (2025-12-11) created a DOJ AI Litigation Task Force. Its first action was intervening against Colorado in xAI v. Weiser, No. 1:26-cv-01515 (D. Colo., filed 2026-04-09) — DOJ filed its Complaint in Intervention 2026-04-24.","confidence":"medium","source":"Case caption and filing dates are primary-sourced (see Colorado's record); the Executive Order's own date and text are an internal note, public source pending. Independently corroborated: Executive Order 14365 established a Justice Department task force to challenge state AI laws, while an attempted 10-year moratorium on state AI laws was defeated in the Senate — Tristan Ingold, AI Frontiers, 2026-03-27 · https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/the-robot-in-your-living-room-has-no-rulebook"},{"summary":"The Great American AI Act (Obernolte / Trahan) would preempt state AI development laws for three years. It is a discussion draft only — not introduced, not passed, not law. One of the draft's most consequential provisions is its three-year preemption clause, whose preemptive effect would likely be greatest for state laws focused on frontier model development, such as California's SB 53, New York's RAISE Act, and Illinois SB 315. The Great American AI Act is still a discussion draft; whether it will advance in its current form remains uncertain.","confidence":"low","source":"Justine Gluck, Future of Privacy Forum, 2026-06-09 · https://fpf.org/blog/frontier-ai-goes-federal-how-the-great-american-ai-act-compares-to-state-laws/"}]},"citations":[{"source":"sb53.info — California Senate Bill 53, annotated","authors":"Miles Kodama (AI Futures Project)","url":"https://sb53.info/","used_in":"Map view: formerly the opening before/after sentence in How to read this map (removed 14:54, owner rewrote the explainer in his own framing). Enforcement cell: private actors have no standing, only the California AG can bring suit (§ 22757.15(b) annotation). Internal-use cell: a model is vulnerable to theft and to misuse by the developer's own staff even before deployment (§ 22757.12(a)(10) annotation). Also the source cited on most California provision rows.","note":"The site footer reads: Content: All rights reserved (the MIT grant covers design and code only). Passages are used here as short attributed quotation for commentary; written permission has NOT been obtained, and should be before anything public ships."},{"source":"Secure AI Project — Policy Priorities (About)","authors":"Secure AI Project (unsigned institutional copy)","url":"https://secureaiproject.org/about","used_in":"Map view, The gaps are the headline: the sentence that no requirement makes company safety practices reasonable or best-practice, or has anyone outside the company check that they are followed. That one sentence carries two of the map's three gaps.","note":""},{"source":"Transformer — OpenAI hack reveals internal deployment risk","authors":"Thomas Woodside (Secure AI Project), quoted by Transformer staff","url":"https://www.transformernews.ai/p/openai-hack-reveals-internal-deployment-risk","used_in":"Map view, Substantive standards gap: nothing requires those safety practices to meet any minimum bar for quality. Substantive minimum-bar cell: the same line plus the enforcement closer — there is no way, in any of these laws, for anyone to say what you're doing is not enough, or that something dangerous needs to stop.","note":"Attributed by name and organisation only. Seven sources give Woodside seven different titles, so the site asserts none. The article carries a July 28 note that a Woodside quote was adjusted; the post-adjustment text is what is used."},{"source":"Office of Gov. JB Pritzker — signing release for SB 315","authors":"Nick Beckstead (Secure AI Project), quoted in the Governor's release","url":"https://gov-pritzker-newsroom.prezly.com/gov-pritzker-signs-nation-leading-artificial-intelligence-safety-law","used_in":"Illinois panel headline: Illinois is the first state to mandate independent third-party evaluations of AI safety practices, making its AI law the strongest in the country.","note":"The audit mechanics stated alongside the quote (annual, public high-level summary, 2028-01-01 start) are ours, primary-verified against Public Act 104-0538, Sec. 10(d)."},{"source":"Office of Gov. JB Pritzker — signing release for SB 315","authors":"Sunny Gandhi (Encode AI), quoted in the Governor's release","url":"https://gov-pritzker-newsroom.prezly.com/gov-pritzker-signs-nation-leading-artificial-intelligence-safety-law","used_in":"Independent verification cell: Illinois builds on the frameworks California and New York have already passed and goes one step further by requiring independent audits, so the public doesn't have to take AI companies at their word.","note":""},{"source":"SB 53 Coalition Letter to Gov. Newsom (23 signatory organizations)","authors":"Encode AI, Design It For Us, Common Sense Media, The Midas Project, CITED, CAIDP and others (organizational letter, no individual byline)","url":"https://encodeai.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/SB-53-Coalition-Letter-9_24_2025.pdf","used_in":"Category definitions for the four SB 53 pillars: frontier AI framework (write, publish, follow, and the choice not to prescribe technical standards), transparency reports (pre-deployment catastrophic-risk assessment results), critical incident reporting (a tightly defined set of reportable incidents), and whistleblower protections (reporting catastrophic risks as well as violations of SB 53 itself).","note":"NOT used: the letter names the Attorney General as the recipient of critical safety incident reports. The enacted statute routes them to the Office of Emergency Services, so that clause is dropped and the recipient is taken from FPF and the statute."},{"source":"Future of Privacy Forum — California's SB 53: The First Frontier AI Law, Explained","authors":"Justine Gluck (Policy Analyst, AI Policy and Legislation, FPF)","url":"https://fpf.org/blog/californias-sb-53-the-first-frontier-ai-law-explained/","used_in":"California panel headline: SB 53 makes California the first state to enact a statute specifically targeting frontier AI safety and transparency. Critical incident reporting cell: reports go to the Office of Emergency Services, which must also establish a mechanism for the public to report incidents.","note":""},{"source":"Future of Privacy Forum — The RAISE Act vs. SB 53: A Tale of Two Frontier AI Laws","authors":"Justine Gluck (FPF)","url":"https://fpf.org/blog/the-raise-act-vs-sb-53-a-tale-of-two-frontier-ai-laws/","used_in":"New York panel headline, as corroboration only: the removal of the deployment prohibition and the scaling back of penalties from $10M/$30M to $1M/$3M. Scope cell: the four-limb catastrophic-risk definition (50 or more deaths or serious injuries or $1B in damages; CBRN uplift; autonomous criminal conduct or cyberattack; evading developer control).","note":"The map's New York facts are primary-verified against the enacted Art. 44-B text; this piece supports them but does not replace them. FPF's separate February 2026 chart is NOT used for SB 53 — it overstates the catastrophic-harm headcount threshold, which the statute sets at 50 or more."},{"source":"Future of Privacy Forum — Frontier AI Goes Federal: How the Great American AI Act Compares to State Laws","authors":"Justine Gluck (FPF)","url":"https://fpf.org/blog/frontier-ai-goes-federal-how-the-great-american-ai-act-compares-to-state-laws/","used_in":"Scope cell: the shared definitions of large frontier developer, frontier model (over 10^26 computational operations) and catastrophic risk. Federal status: the three-year preemption clause, whose effect would fall hardest on SB 53, RAISE and SB 315, and the fact that the Act remains a discussion draft.","note":""},{"source":"Future of Privacy Forum — SB 5 in Five: What to Know About Connecticut's New AI Law","authors":"Justine Gluck and Daniel Hales (Policy Counsel for U.S. Legislation, FPF)","url":"https://fpf.org/blog/sb-5-in-five-what-to-know-about-connecticuts-new-ai-law/","used_in":"Connecticut panel headline: SB 5 borrows the language of frontier AI laws but not the full architecture — it defines the terms, requires none of the frameworks, reports or incident mechanisms, and protects employees who report serious AI-related risks.","note":"Connecticut's signing date (2026-05-27) is primary-verified against the CGA bill-status page, not taken from this post's relative 'this week' phrasing."},{"source":"Future of Privacy Forum — Colorado Revises its AI Act: What Changed and Why","authors":"Tatiana Rice (Senior Director for U.S. Legislation, FPF)","url":"https://fpf.org/blog/colorado-revises-its-ai-act-what-changed-and-why/","used_in":"Colorado panel headline: the replacement law eliminates the 2024 Act's governance requirements and references to algorithmic discrimination, focusing instead on transparency.","note":"Date conflict resolved against the source: FPF gives a signing date one day later than the enrolled act. The map uses the enrolled-act date, 2026-05-14."},{"source":"Future of Privacy Forum — Enacted AI Legislation Chart","authors":"Future of Privacy Forum (institutional, no individual byline)","url":"https://fpf.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Enacted-AI-Legislation-Chart-.pdf","used_in":"Texas panel headline (TRAIGA's prohibited purposes, government-use rules, 36-month sandbox, AI Advisory Council) and Utah panel headline (AIPA disclosure duties, sandbox and Office of AI Policy; SB 226's high-risk narrowing; SB 332's extension to July 2027).","note":"NOT used: the chart's Colorado entry describes the never-effective SB 24-205 in the present tense, and its SB 53 entry misstates the catastrophic-harm threshold. Only the Texas and Utah entries are borrowed."},{"source":"AI Frontiers — The Hidden AI Frontier","authors":"Oscar Delaney and Ashwin Acharya","url":"https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/the-hidden-ai-frontier","used_in":"Map view, Internal-use transparency gap (the lead gap): internal models are the first to develop dual-use capabilities in cyberoffense and bioweapon design, the dangers they may pose are invisible to the public, policymakers and third-party auditors, and policymakers are flying blind. Internal-use cell: the same dual-use point plus the widening internal-versus-public capability gap.","note":""},{"source":"AI Frontiers — How the EU's Code of Practice Advances AI Safety","authors":"Henry Papadatos (Managing Director, SaferAI)","url":"https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/how-the-eus-code-of-practice-advances-ai-safety","used_in":"Internal-use cell rationale, as the contrast case: the EU Code does not cover internally deployed models, the AI Act reaches only models placed on the EU market, and that is a crucial gap for loss-of-control scenarios.","note":""},{"source":"AI Frontiers — Don't Let AI Developers Hire Their Own Referees","authors":"Gabriel Weil (University of Houston Law Center; Institute for Law & AI)","url":"https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/dont-let-ai-developers-hire-their-own-referees","used_in":"Independent verification cell, as the counterpoint to Illinois' audit mandate: a verifier that depends on the developers it clears for repeat business has an incentive to grade gently, and competition drives the model toward laxity.","note":"Kept deliberately, so the Illinois cell does not read as boosterism. Weil's term IVO is rendered as independent verifier for readability; the argument is unchanged."},{"source":"AI Frontiers — Catalytic Regulation: Incentivizing Safety During a Regulatory Drought","authors":"Yonathan Arbel (law professor, University of Alabama)","url":"https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/catalytic-regulation-incentivizing-safety-during-a-regulatory-drought","used_in":"Substantive minimum-bar cell definition: there is little organized political appetite for, and strong industry pressure against, command-and-control measures such as mandates, prohibitions and prescriptive safety requirements — the political fact behind the empty cell.","note":""},{"source":"AI Frontiers — Congress Might Block States From Regulating AI","authors":"Kristin O'Donoghue (Carnegie Endowment for International Peace)","url":"https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/congress-might-block-states-from-regulating-ai","used_in":"Federal status, first item: the proposed moratorium was a prohibition rather than preemption, with little historical precedent — knocking out state and local laws without a federal law to fill the void.","note":""},{"source":"AI Frontiers — The Robot in Your Living Room Has No Rulebook","authors":"Tristan Ingold","url":"https://ai-frontiers.org/articles/the-robot-in-your-living-room-has-no-rulebook","used_in":"Federal status, second item, as independent corroboration of the map's internal note: Executive Order 14365 established a Justice Department task force to challenge state AI laws, and the attempted 10-year moratorium was defeated in the Senate.","note":"NOT used: the same paragraph gives Colorado's 2024 AI Act a mid-2026 effective date. That law never took effect and was replaced on 2026-05-14, seven weeks before the piece ran."},{"source":"State legislature texts — enrolled acts and official bill text","authors":"California Legislature (SB 53), New York State Legislature (S6953-B/A6453-B as amended by S8828), Illinois General Assembly (SB 315 / Public Act 104-0538), Connecticut General Assembly (SB 5 / Public Act 26-15), Colorado General Assembly (SB 24-205, SB 26-189), Texas Legislature (HB 149), Utah Legislature (SB 149 and amendments)","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53","used_in":"Every provision summary and statutory cite on every state panel. Each bill row links its own official text, and the provision rows carry their own source lines. Where a borrowed passage and the statute disagree, the statute wins and the borrowed passage is dropped.","note":"Per-bill links are on each state panel; this entry records the class of source rather than one URL."}]};
